WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.985
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.021 --> 00:00:10.359
Welcome, my name is Jessica Talbert and

00:00:10.359 --> 00:00:14.085
I'm a second year MBA at
Stanford Graduate School of Business.

00:00:14.085 --> 00:00:17.451
And I'm here today with Kirsten Moss,
Assistant Dean and

00:00:17.451 --> 00:00:20.001
Director of Admissions and Financial Aid.

00:00:20.001 --> 00:00:20.958
&gt;&gt; Hi, Jessica.

00:00:20.958 --> 00:00:22.386
Thanks for the introduction.

00:00:22.386 --> 00:00:26.533
As admissions officers, we spend much of
our time traveling around the world and

00:00:26.533 --> 00:00:29.835
meeting with candidates to share
information about Stanford.

00:00:29.835 --> 00:00:32.759
And whether or not it could be a good
fit for what they're looking for

00:00:32.759 --> 00:00:34.620
in their graduate education experience.

00:00:34.620 --> 00:00:36.705
But today we want to do
something different.

00:00:36.705 --> 00:00:40.471
Instead of talking about Stanford,
we want to talk about you and how you if

00:00:40.471 --> 00:00:44.573
you're interested in applying can put
your best foot forward in the process.

00:00:44.573 --> 00:00:49.132
&gt;&gt; What are some of the most common myths
about the Stanford application process?

00:00:49.132 --> 00:00:52.509
&gt;&gt; I think the myth that I hear
most frequently is that members of

00:00:52.509 --> 00:00:56.619
the Admissions Committee at Stanford
have some magic checklist that each

00:00:56.619 --> 00:01:00.153
applicant must fulfill in order
to be admitted to our program.

00:01:00.153 --> 00:01:03.624
I've heard things like I have to
have a GMAT score over a 700, or

00:01:03.624 --> 00:01:06.600
I need to work in a particular
industry to be admitted, or

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:09.902
even come from a particular type
of educational institution.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:12.512
And really nothing could
be farther from the truth.

00:01:12.512 --> 00:01:15.530
If you think about the last few
classes that we've admitted,

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:18.211
we have students from over
60 different countries.

00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:22.155
We have students from 160 different
educational institutions.

00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:25.925
And we have students coming from I think
on average over 300 different work

00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:28.723
environments before they come,
different companies.

00:01:28.723 --> 00:01:31.189
And there's only 400 students in a class.

00:01:31.189 --> 00:01:35.642
So you can see in reality, we're admitting
humans for what they've done, and

00:01:35.642 --> 00:01:36.450
who they are.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:40.361
We're not admitting people just because
they went to a certain institution, or

00:01:40.361 --> 00:01:41.870
worked in a certain industry.

00:01:41.870 --> 00:01:45.134
In fact, I was just, I think it
was a few weekends ago, we were at

00:01:45.134 --> 00:01:49.027
Admitted Students Weekend, which I
know you've been to, Admit Weekend.

00:01:49.027 --> 00:01:51.011
And there were two
students I was talking to.

00:01:51.011 --> 00:01:55.200
And one of them said that, both of them
said they almost didn't apply to Stanford.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.858
I came this close to not applying,
I'm like, why?

00:01:57.858 --> 00:02:01.994
One said, he was in a chatroom,
and somebody told him that,

00:02:01.994 --> 00:02:06.959
Stanford would never admit you because
of the university that you went to, and

00:02:06.959 --> 00:02:09.538
he went to a state school in the Midwest.

00:02:09.538 --> 00:02:12.831
And then another student said
the same thing that happen to her.

00:02:12.831 --> 00:02:16.841
And one of our peers from work had told
her, they are talking about where they

00:02:16.841 --> 00:02:21.057
wanted to apply that with her GPA and
test score should never get into Stanford.

00:02:21.057 --> 00:02:25.583
So luckily for us, both of these students
decided that for themselves whether or

00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:29.179
not they should apply and
are now going to be entering this fall, so

00:02:29.179 --> 00:02:31.195
we're really excited about that.

00:02:31.195 --> 00:02:35.330
&gt;&gt; How do you decide then whether or not
you have a shot at getting into Stanford?

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:38.164
&gt;&gt; That's a great question, that's
probably the million dollar question.

00:02:38.164 --> 00:02:42.953
And I'm hoping all what we discussed today
will give applicants insight into whether

00:02:42.953 --> 00:02:44.297
or not they have a shot.

00:02:44.297 --> 00:02:48.744
But I think if I had to go to the essence
like what is absolutely most important

00:02:48.744 --> 00:02:50.427
than making that decision.

00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:54.032
We're trying to answer two questions
on the Admissions Committee.

00:02:54.032 --> 00:02:58.738
The first is, is this particular applicant
going to be engaged academically when

00:02:58.738 --> 00:03:00.294
they come to our program?

00:03:00.294 --> 00:03:04.432
And the second is, what kind of impact
will they have after they graduate?

00:03:04.432 --> 00:03:09.046
And if you think, well, we care about
their educational engagement because we're

00:03:09.046 --> 00:03:12.952
a higher education institution,
we want our classroom to be vibrant.

00:03:12.952 --> 00:03:16.721
You probably know as a student, the more
interested folks are when they're in there

00:03:16.721 --> 00:03:19.817
learning and curious the better and
richer that experience will be.

00:03:19.817 --> 00:03:23.650
Then on the flip side, we do care and
believe in our tagline.

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.914
We want to know when we're selecting these
students that they have the potential to

00:03:27.914 --> 00:03:31.210
change lives, change organizations,
and change the world.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:35.792
Jessica, I'm curious about how you
decided whether to apply to Stanford?

00:03:35.792 --> 00:03:39.702
&gt;&gt; For me, it was about thinking
am I ready to take the next

00:03:39.702 --> 00:03:42.511
leap in my career and not the just step?

00:03:42.511 --> 00:03:46.146
I've spent about five years in retail and
tech, and kind of formulated an idea of

00:03:46.146 --> 00:03:49.090
the kind of impact I wanted
to have on those industries.

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:52.780
But I wasn't exactly sure what the best
step forward was in order to be

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.130
successful.

00:03:54.130 --> 00:03:58.653
I think knowing that I was ready to make
that impact is what helped me decide to

00:03:58.653 --> 00:03:59.157
apply.

00:03:59.157 --> 00:04:02.372
&gt;&gt; It's great, how did you
approach the application process?

00:04:02.372 --> 00:04:03.341
&gt;&gt; The application process?

00:04:03.341 --> 00:04:07.185
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I think I thought about it in two ways

00:04:07.185 --> 00:04:07.876
mainly.

00:04:07.876 --> 00:04:10.695
The first one was really
about introspection.

00:04:10.695 --> 00:04:14.911
Really understanding who I was,
what I wanted to get out of the world and

00:04:14.911 --> 00:04:16.605
what kind of impact I wanted.

00:04:16.605 --> 00:04:18.802
And it's easier said than done.

00:04:18.802 --> 00:04:21.995
For me, it took the form of journaling
every morning and then kind of

00:04:21.995 --> 00:04:25.827
looking back after a couple of months and
seeing what common themes there were.

00:04:25.827 --> 00:04:28.470
What was important to me
on a day to day basis?

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:31.701
And I think once I crystallized around
that I had a really good idea of like,

00:04:31.701 --> 00:04:33.819
what I really wanted to get out of it and
who I was.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:39.537
The second part was about
information gathering.

00:04:39.537 --> 00:04:42.569
Making sure that what I wanted to get out
of it was actually where business school

00:04:42.569 --> 00:04:44.977
is going to be like and not just what
I thought it was going to be like.

00:04:44.977 --> 00:04:48.785
And that was coming through
Stanford's resources,

00:04:48.785 --> 00:04:53.192
going to town halls that your
office would go to around the US.

00:04:53.192 --> 00:04:56.097
And really talking to alumni and
current students.

00:04:56.097 --> 00:04:58.866
Can we talk about how you
evaluate whether someone will

00:04:58.866 --> 00:05:00.680
engage academically at Stanford?

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:04.124
&gt;&gt; We're looking for students who
are curious, who are willing to

00:05:04.124 --> 00:05:08.400
share their perspectives both inside and
outside of the classroom.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:11.332
And perhaps most importantly
we're looking for

00:05:11.332 --> 00:05:15.835
students who want to seek new ideas,
new perspectives, almost insights or

00:05:15.835 --> 00:05:18.638
truth, and
to do that in a collaborative way.

00:05:18.638 --> 00:05:21.201
When you leave GSB as a leader,
you're going to go out and

00:05:21.201 --> 00:05:24.793
have some tough problems to solve, but
you're not going to do it by yourself.

00:05:24.793 --> 00:05:26.704
You're not going to do it
in your own head somewhere.

00:05:26.704 --> 00:05:30.301
So really having someone who'd going to be
leveraging the thoughts of others to come

00:05:30.301 --> 00:05:32.500
up with the best possible
solution is important.

00:05:33.740 --> 00:05:38.240
To assess these behaviors, we look at
multiple places in the application.

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.239
The most obvious would be the transcript.

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:43.317
And for me, I usually read,
I'll take a day, Tuesdays and

00:05:43.317 --> 00:05:46.653
Thursdays during application season,
I get to read from home.

00:05:46.653 --> 00:05:48.167
My favorite thing to do, and

00:05:48.167 --> 00:05:52.405
every time my [LAUGH] I get that really
long with the applicants and your stories.

00:05:52.405 --> 00:05:56.104
But when I open a file, one of first thing
I do is I go to the transcript because I

00:05:56.104 --> 00:05:57.576
tell so much about someone.

00:05:57.576 --> 00:06:01.835
What are the kind of courses you took,
what are you most interested in studying.

00:06:01.835 --> 00:06:06.078
Are you someone who went broad, I like to
see how different the discipline connect

00:06:06.078 --> 00:06:08.920
with each other,
ot maybe someone who were very deep.

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:13.617
So that's step one, and
I do get information about how curious

00:06:13.617 --> 00:06:18.324
someone is and how the rigor and
course choices impacted them.

00:06:18.324 --> 00:06:21.996
But perhaps more importantly than
the transcript is looking for

00:06:21.996 --> 00:06:25.873
evidence of this curiosity and
other things that people have done.

00:06:25.873 --> 00:06:27.359
So perhaps you were at work and

00:06:27.359 --> 00:06:31.289
there's a tough problems to solve and
you worked with others to figure it out.

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:35.238
Or perhaps you did research at your
university beyond your classes.

00:06:35.238 --> 00:06:40.650
One of my most recent favorite stories
was from someone we admit in this class,

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:42.502
and then-
&gt;&gt; It wasn't like.

00:06:42.502 --> 00:06:47.260
&gt;&gt; No it wasn't yours, sorry,
Jessica, it could have been though.

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:51.500
And it was an individual, I think he was
in business development in a tech company.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:53.225
It was a growing tech company, and

00:06:53.225 --> 00:06:55.968
he joined when the company
was only about 50 people.

00:06:55.968 --> 00:06:59.656
And he said one of the things he loved
best when he joined was that wherever

00:06:59.656 --> 00:07:01.841
he was there was laughter in the hallways.

00:07:01.841 --> 00:07:05.944
And he thought that was a sign of
people being optimistic, innovative.

00:07:05.944 --> 00:07:08.837
And then as time went on and
they were doubling and

00:07:08.837 --> 00:07:11.317
tripling in size the laughter went away.

00:07:11.317 --> 00:07:14.456
And he remembered going to
an all hands meeting and

00:07:14.456 --> 00:07:18.504
asked the senior management,
this is a concern, what can I do?

00:07:18.504 --> 00:07:21.283
And came up with the idea
to do a pulse survey.

00:07:21.283 --> 00:07:23.381
The only problem with to
check in with the culture.

00:07:23.381 --> 00:07:26.862
The only problem was he said he had
no idea how to do an HR survey,

00:07:26.862 --> 00:07:31.687
what cultural engagement was, how to build
it, how to write questions, all of that.

00:07:31.687 --> 00:07:35.437
But you watch him go through a path of
just curiosity of meeting with experts,

00:07:35.437 --> 00:07:37.760
both in the company and
outside of the company.

00:07:37.760 --> 00:07:41.579
Researching material to figure out, yeah,
what are some good benchmark surveys,

00:07:41.579 --> 00:07:43.586
how do we develop questions that'll work?

00:07:43.586 --> 00:07:47.445
Luckily they had some PhD students in this
company who were doing business analytics

00:07:47.445 --> 00:07:48.707
that I could get help from.

00:07:48.707 --> 00:07:53.649
But the point is, that's a great
story about using your curiosity and

00:07:53.649 --> 00:07:57.857
your drive to find truth about
this company to come up with.

00:07:57.857 --> 00:08:00.853
They essentially came up with a great
answer about what they could be doing

00:08:00.853 --> 00:08:03.240
differently as they move forward, so.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:05.620
Hopefully, that helps give you
a sense of what I'm looking for and

00:08:05.620 --> 00:08:06.430
how I'm looking for it.

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:12.340
&gt;&gt; Many students, myself included,
are worried about their test scores.

00:08:12.340 --> 00:08:14.540
What advice do you have there?

00:08:14.540 --> 00:08:18.200
&gt;&gt; This is probably the most
frequently asked question that I get is

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.570
what about my GMAT score,
what about my GRE?

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:26.120
And the reality is we accept students

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:31.250
with a wide range of test scores, and is
just one data point and I truly mean that.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:36.890
Well, we look at the tests and they are
important to ensure that you're going to

00:08:36.890 --> 00:08:41.380
be able to thrive quantitatively and
verbally in the program.

00:08:41.380 --> 00:08:45.340
So my best advice is we look at it,
you should take spend some time preparing.

00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:48.250
What I hope for every applicant is
when they submit that score and

00:08:48.250 --> 00:08:49.460
I know it's stressful,

00:08:49.460 --> 00:08:53.720
that you feel it's at least a decent
reflection of what your capabilities are.

00:08:53.720 --> 00:08:55.760
So that's where the preparation comes in.

00:08:55.760 --> 00:09:00.480
That being said, though, I know for many
students test taking is not their forte.

00:09:00.480 --> 00:09:02.340
So again, if there's a score, honestly,

00:09:02.340 --> 00:09:07.440
in an application where it's far from
maybe what our median might be and

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:11.130
we do admit people in the 500s,
the 600s, and the 700s.

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.502
But if I see this may not be the strongest
point of their application, that's when

00:09:14.502 --> 00:09:17.620
I'm diving in into the transcript and
seeing again, how do they learn?

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:18.804
Maybe tests just aren't their thing.

00:09:20.884 --> 00:09:22.840
&gt;&gt; So I'd like to ask you about GPA.

00:09:22.840 --> 00:09:25.470
Stanford says that there's no minimum GPA.

00:09:25.470 --> 00:09:27.850
But can you get in with a low GPA?

00:09:27.850 --> 00:09:30.880
I'd also like to know how you
evaluate GPAs outside of the US?

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:35.261
&gt;&gt; Both of those questions come up
from applicants often, as well.

00:09:35.261 --> 00:09:40.128
Again, the GPA is just one indicator,
but this one is about a four-year period

00:09:40.128 --> 00:09:43.410
of time, often a three or
four-year period of time.

00:09:43.410 --> 00:09:45.082
So we do look at that.

00:09:45.082 --> 00:09:49.394
For us, if you think about the GPA,
we know that it's going to vary by

00:09:49.394 --> 00:09:53.090
institution, the level of the GPA,
also even by major.

00:09:53.090 --> 00:09:57.060
Sometimes I find that highly rigorous
quantitative majors or sometimes

00:09:57.060 --> 00:10:00.620
more science oriented, on average,
students' GPA will be lower there.

00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:03.190
So that's something we're
taking into consideration.

00:10:03.190 --> 00:10:07.457
In addition,
if you think about international schools,

00:10:07.457 --> 00:10:11.210
we don't convert their
GPAs into US conversions.

00:10:11.210 --> 00:10:12.940
We actually look at the country and

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:16.708
try to be familiar with what's their
educational system and recognize that

00:10:16.708 --> 00:10:21.500
oftentimes international GPAs are less
inflated than US GPAs may be.

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:25.660
So all of that is for
you to realize that we're trying to do our

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:29.240
best to understand what they looks
like in your particular context.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:31.910
That being said,
if you look at your transcript and

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:34.320
take it look at it with fresh
eyes on an applicant, and

00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:37.920
you see a point in time, a semester,
quarter or whatever it was where

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.470
you don't think it reflects your true
capabilities, tell us about that.

00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:43.570
We have an additional information section.

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:44.480
It could've been health.

00:10:44.480 --> 00:10:46.370
It could've been a personal situation.

00:10:46.370 --> 00:10:50.248
We're looking for reasons to admit you,
not for reasons to deny.

00:10:50.248 --> 00:10:53.380
So any contexts that you can
give us to help understand

00:10:53.380 --> 00:10:56.090
what your trajectory has
been will be really helpful.

00:10:56.090 --> 00:10:57.889
That's great.
I like the framing of that too.

00:10:57.889 --> 00:11:00.476
We're looking for
reasons to admit us, not to deny us.

00:11:00.476 --> 00:11:01.418
&gt;&gt; Exactly.

00:11:01.418 --> 00:11:04.100
&gt;&gt; And no applicant is going to be
outstanding in every dimension.

00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:05.890
So you put your best foot forward and

00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:07.810
know that those strengths
are the important things.

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:09.750
&gt;&gt; Let's discuss letters of reference.

00:11:09.750 --> 00:11:11.535
What advice do you have for
choosing recommenders?

00:11:14.190 --> 00:11:17.550
&gt;&gt; I don't know if applicants really
appreciate how important the letters of

00:11:17.550 --> 00:11:18.890
reference are.

00:11:18.890 --> 00:11:22.480
It's really the one opportunity that we
get to see you through someone else's

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:23.770
eyes objectively.

00:11:23.770 --> 00:11:25.620
What did you do and how did you do it?

00:11:25.620 --> 00:11:29.440
So, for me, put a lot of
emphasis on making a selection.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:31.740
I can give you some tips
on how to think about it.

00:11:31.740 --> 00:11:36.350
At Stanford, we request two and one we
ask for a current direct supervisor or

00:11:37.460 --> 00:11:40.830
the best alternative if that is
impossible or inappropriate.

00:11:40.830 --> 00:11:43.200
And then for the second recommendation,
we also ask for

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:44.540
someone who supervised your work.

00:11:44.540 --> 00:11:45.260
But it could be for

00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:48.865
a non-profit, it could be extracurricular
or from the same organization.

00:11:48.865 --> 00:11:51.070
Now, when you think about selecting them,

00:11:51.070 --> 00:11:55.620
we really just as I've asked applicants in
their essays to tell us about the impact

00:11:55.620 --> 00:11:59.240
that they've had, because this past
behavior will predict future behavior,

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.750
that's the echo that we
want to see in the reference.

00:12:01.750 --> 00:12:05.600
So find someone who knows what you've
done, really been in the trenches and

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.680
can see your work and describe not just
what happened but how you did it, and

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.310
how the way you did it might
have been different from others.

00:12:12.310 --> 00:12:14.570
So that means don't worry
about the title of a person,

00:12:14.570 --> 00:12:16.500
don't try to get the highest title.

00:12:16.500 --> 00:12:19.400
Also, I hear a lot of students asking or
applicants asking,

00:12:19.400 --> 00:12:22.990
should I try to find someone who went
to Stanford because that's important?

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:23.740
No, it's not,

00:12:23.740 --> 00:12:29.940
it's actually what the recommender can
tell us about you that is most important.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:33.410
The second thing is after them
understanding what you've done is

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:34.740
enthusiasm.

00:12:34.740 --> 00:12:39.790
This is [LAUGH] hard for someone who
reads thousands of recommendations.

00:12:39.790 --> 00:12:42.390
This one's a hard one to
wrap your hands around.

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:48.294
But I know the best recommendations that I
read are written from both a headspace and

00:12:48.294 --> 00:12:49.410
a heart space.

00:12:49.410 --> 00:12:52.150
So try to think about who you've
established a relationship with,

00:12:52.150 --> 00:12:55.322
someone who really
enthusiasm a great work,

00:12:55.322 --> 00:12:58.440
but has a connection with you and
will be able to speak to that.

00:12:58.440 --> 00:13:01.750
And that's how recommendations
become most memorable.

00:13:01.750 --> 00:13:04.770
And then lastly, I think applicants
should think about the process.

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.312
Don't ask for recommendation in an email.

00:13:06.312 --> 00:13:10.410
It's going to take this person
a significant amount of time.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:12.550
On average,
our references are about two pages.

00:13:12.550 --> 00:13:17.690
I'm guessing people has to spend at least
an hour or two to do a good job on them.

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:23.100
What is most disappointing to me, when I'm
really looking to find reasons to admit,

00:13:23.100 --> 00:13:26.360
is I'll get to the end of an application,
I'll be on the second recommendation and

00:13:26.360 --> 00:13:29.235
I'll see someone everything has been so
positive.

00:13:29.235 --> 00:13:31.185
And they'll write a positive
recommendation, but

00:13:31.185 --> 00:13:34.245
maybe only two paragraphs, and
just say, they're phenomenal.

00:13:34.245 --> 00:13:35.445
They're the best ever.

00:13:35.445 --> 00:13:36.495
They've done a great job.

00:13:36.495 --> 00:13:39.605
But I won't have any of that
data to help me make a decision.

00:13:39.605 --> 00:13:44.680
And it just means that this particular
applicant doesn't have as much data,

00:13:44.680 --> 00:13:46.360
I guess, is the best word,
but landscape for

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:49.520
me to really understand what they've
done and how they've done it.

00:13:49.520 --> 00:13:51.010
So take it seriously.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.110
Make an appointment,
even if it's a 15 minute chat.

00:13:53.110 --> 00:13:56.770
But ask the question courageously and
say, will you write it?

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.200
Explain why, where you're going,
what schools.

00:13:59.200 --> 00:14:02.890
And then say, do you have time to
invest to do a good job on this?

00:14:02.890 --> 00:14:04.200
And if someone says they're really busy,

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:07.095
that a good indication that maybe
you should go to your second choice.

00:14:07.095 --> 00:14:09.831
because it can make a difference
in the end of how much they're

00:14:09.831 --> 00:14:12.228
willing to spend time to
help you in this process.

00:14:12.228 --> 00:14:15.390
&gt;&gt; And then for the recommenders,
are there any resources for them or

00:14:15.390 --> 00:14:18.510
how do they know how to
write then to get the best?

00:14:18.510 --> 00:14:20.163
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:14:20.163 --> 00:14:24.518
We try to give, and many recommenders
have written more than one before, but

00:14:24.518 --> 00:14:26.461
we try to give them clear advice and

00:14:26.461 --> 00:14:30.010
tips when they're invited onto
our portal on the website.

00:14:30.010 --> 00:14:32.750
The questions will ask,
how has this person differentiated

00:14:32.750 --> 00:14:36.730
themselves from others that
they've seen doing similar roles?

00:14:36.730 --> 00:14:38.210
And what have they done and accomplished?

00:14:38.210 --> 00:14:40.365
So they'll get that.

00:14:40.365 --> 00:14:45.268
And I think it comes down to that
they're willing to take the time to

00:14:45.268 --> 00:14:46.855
really support you.

00:14:46.855 --> 00:14:50.031
There's no magic there, but
they do have to make that investment and

00:14:50.031 --> 00:14:53.726
be close enough to you to be able to write
thoughtfully about what you've done.

00:14:53.726 --> 00:14:57.988
&gt;&gt; Yeah, many reapplicants worry that
they are disadvantaged to first time

00:14:57.988 --> 00:14:59.530
applicants.

00:14:59.530 --> 00:15:00.800
Is that true?

00:15:00.800 --> 00:15:03.081
And do you have any advice for
people are trying to reapply?

00:15:03.081 --> 00:15:07.660
&gt;&gt; We really read them fresh, so my best
advice is the person who is evaluating

00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:09.990
your application likely
has never seen it before.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:11.760
So there's no reason to
explain what's different or

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:14.400
what changed or
maybe why you think you didn't get in.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:16.390
Some people will try to tell us that.

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.570
Just start fresh and
look at what you've already submitted.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:19.880
You don't have to change everything.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:22.810
But think is there something at this
moment in time that could be more

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:24.486
compelling, perhaps, that the last?

00:15:24.486 --> 00:15:27.460
Or some way you want to change it to
be more representative of where I am at

00:15:27.460 --> 00:15:28.720
this moment.

00:15:28.720 --> 00:15:31.897
But please do,
if you're committed to Stanford, reapply,

00:15:31.897 --> 00:15:35.206
because there's a lot of students
that we admit from that category.

00:15:35.206 --> 00:15:36.966
&gt;&gt; Some of my favorite
classmates are reapplicants, so-

00:15:36.966 --> 00:15:37.473
&gt;&gt; They told you?

00:15:37.473 --> 00:15:38.026
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So I'm really

00:15:38.026 --> 00:15:38.575
glad-
&gt;&gt; That's great.

00:15:38.575 --> 00:15:40.105
&gt;&gt; That they're here.

00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:41.530
&gt;&gt; That's awesome.

00:15:42.780 --> 00:15:46.200
&gt;&gt; If an applicant does get an interview,
how would you recommend they prepare for

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.680
that interview?

00:15:47.680 --> 00:15:50.770
&gt;&gt; Well, first of all, if an applicant
gets an interview, congratulations.

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:51.652
That's a really good sign.

00:15:51.652 --> 00:15:53.277
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's awesome.

00:15:53.277 --> 00:15:56.860
&gt;&gt; We usually interview about two
candidates for every spot that we have.

00:15:56.860 --> 00:15:59.750
So number one,
the word prepare is a really good one.

00:15:59.750 --> 00:16:00.860
If you've made it this far and

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:05.420
worked this hard I treat this is as
an important part of the process.

00:16:07.230 --> 00:16:09.930
Unlike some other schools,
we use our alumni for interviewers and

00:16:09.930 --> 00:16:11.130
they are all around the globe.

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:15.960
We try to match it with people from your
country, maybe perhaps an industry that

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:19.220
you're in, if possible currently in,
so they really understand you.

00:16:19.220 --> 00:16:20.210
And it's in two parts.

00:16:20.210 --> 00:16:23.200
The first half an hour
is primarily evaluative.

00:16:23.200 --> 00:16:25.070
And then the last 15 minutes,
it's a chance for

00:16:25.070 --> 00:16:27.490
you to meet someone from the community.

00:16:27.490 --> 00:16:31.585
So come prepared with
the mindset to do both.

00:16:31.585 --> 00:16:35.000
In the first part,
it's a behavioral based interview.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.780
So the stories that we were talking
about before, come ready to tell them.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.470
We're going to want to know
about what you're most proud of.

00:16:41.470 --> 00:16:42.640
What was the context?

00:16:42.640 --> 00:16:43.730
What was the challenge?

00:16:43.730 --> 00:16:44.820
What did you do?

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:45.690
How did you do it?

00:16:45.690 --> 00:16:47.890
And then what was the impact?

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:51.580
So expect some probing
around those stories.

00:16:51.580 --> 00:16:54.797
And then on the second part,
it's fun, our community's so tight.

00:16:54.797 --> 00:16:58.720
[LAUGH] I enjoyed my interview
a lot actually, I had a great time.

00:16:58.720 --> 00:17:00.760
&gt;&gt; And did you enjoy the interviewer?

00:17:00.760 --> 00:17:03.230
&gt;&gt; I did and
he was pretty expert, actually.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:06.598
He was really good and
really drawing stories out of me,

00:17:06.598 --> 00:17:09.540
rather than like intense questions.

00:17:09.540 --> 00:17:12.950
By the end of it, I felt that there were
somebody that I'd really gotten to know

00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:15.450
and somebody who is a part of
a community I was excited to be part of.

00:17:15.450 --> 00:17:20.020
So the interview is actually really great
for me and getting excited for school.

00:17:20.020 --> 00:17:23.830
&gt;&gt; And that's a wonderful way
to help with the second tip.

00:17:23.830 --> 00:17:25.840
That last part of the interview,
it's a gift.

00:17:25.840 --> 00:17:27.970
It's a chance to develop a relationship.

00:17:27.970 --> 00:17:31.460
Come prepared with some questions that you
want to know about Stanford, obviously.

00:17:31.460 --> 00:17:36.300
But see it as just one entry step to
being part of a bigger community.

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:39.000
&gt;&gt; So there is a lot of
information out there for

00:17:39.000 --> 00:17:40.460
people applying to business school.

00:17:40.460 --> 00:17:41.945
I know I went through a lot of it.

00:17:41.945 --> 00:17:43.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Some of it helpful, some of it not.

00:17:43.410 --> 00:17:46.740
What are the best resources for
applying to Stanford?

00:17:47.870 --> 00:17:51.990
&gt;&gt; I think you make a good point, there is
a lot of misinformation out there as well.

00:17:51.990 --> 00:17:54.050
We really believe the best information,

00:17:54.050 --> 00:17:57.320
start with what we put
up there on the website.

00:17:57.320 --> 00:18:00.650
What's nice about it now is there's lots
of conversations with students out there,

00:18:00.650 --> 00:18:03.840
so you can learn what it's
actually like from the students.

00:18:03.840 --> 00:18:07.355
I think that's more interesting
than hearing it from admissions.

00:18:07.355 --> 00:18:08.310
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:18:08.310 --> 00:18:12.790
&gt;&gt; As well as there's profile information,
tips about the application, start there.

00:18:12.790 --> 00:18:14.690
But one of the most
important things you can do

00:18:14.690 --> 00:18:16.590
is also go to an information session.

00:18:16.590 --> 00:18:18.310
We do about a hundred of
them around the globe.

00:18:18.310 --> 00:18:19.240
And the reason for

00:18:19.240 --> 00:18:23.240
that is you'll get a panel,
usually three to five recent graduates.

00:18:23.240 --> 00:18:28.450
And they can tell you, we believe that
Stanford can be transformational for

00:18:28.450 --> 00:18:31.820
you both in what you learn,
but also how you develop.

00:18:31.820 --> 00:18:34.320
So you can actually listen
to how this experience has

00:18:34.320 --> 00:18:36.020
impacted people in their own lives.

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:40.150
Both from how they went through as well as
what they're doing now when they graduate.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:43.340
I think that's one of the best
ways you can truly be inspired but

00:18:43.340 --> 00:18:45.150
also get your questions answered.

00:18:45.150 --> 00:18:48.260
And if we're not coming to your hometown,
then go to the Web.

00:18:48.260 --> 00:18:49.320
We have a virtual one too,

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:53.070
where you can hear students talking and
recent alumni about their experiences.

00:18:53.070 --> 00:18:54.040
&gt;&gt; Any other advice?

00:18:54.040 --> 00:18:58.020
&gt;&gt; Before I give you my last words of
wisdom or my last piece of advice.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:19:01.320
We've been spending the last
half an hour talking about

00:19:01.320 --> 00:19:04.030
how the admissions committee
evaluates candidates.

00:19:04.030 --> 00:19:06.420
But I would really like to
put you on the spot and

00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:09.710
say you started two years ago,
you're about to graduate.

00:19:09.710 --> 00:19:10.490
&gt;&gt; Yes.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:11.360
&gt;&gt; Just a few weeks?

00:19:12.360 --> 00:19:16.140
I'm curious what surprised you most
about joining this community of students

00:19:16.140 --> 00:19:16.710
at the GSB?

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:23.508
&gt;&gt; This has just been such
an amazing two years.

00:19:23.508 --> 00:19:28.827
Difficult but amazing, and
I think a couple things surprised me.

00:19:28.827 --> 00:19:31.587
The first one is that I knew that
I was going to come here and

00:19:31.587 --> 00:19:35.610
find really intelligent classmates,
really accomplished classmates.

00:19:35.610 --> 00:19:39.215
People who were going to make
me feel like I didn't do a lot.

00:19:39.215 --> 00:19:40.350
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:19:40.350 --> 00:19:44.680
&gt;&gt; But I don't think I could have fathomed
just the breadth of experience and

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:47.360
passions that these people would have.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.850
And how important that was going to
be to me in my experience here.

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:55.780
I could have a good idea of like what
the academics were going to look like, but

00:19:55.780 --> 00:19:57.200
not really the people I was going to meet.

00:19:57.200 --> 00:20:01.000
And I've met some of the most
amazing people in the world here.

00:20:01.000 --> 00:20:04.120
And for that, I'll be really grateful.

00:20:04.120 --> 00:20:06.840
The second thing that I think was really
surprising to me is that I expected to

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:09.380
become a better business person here.

00:20:09.380 --> 00:20:15.500
But I didn't expect to become a better
person and I really do think that I have.

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:20.180
I think a lot of that I owe to the breadth
of experience from my classmates and

00:20:20.180 --> 00:20:21.620
the time I've spent with them.

00:20:21.620 --> 00:20:23.120
&gt;&gt; What do you mean a better person?

00:20:23.120 --> 00:20:24.080
&gt;&gt; A better person.

00:20:24.080 --> 00:20:25.530
&gt;&gt; That's well said.

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:30.210
&gt;&gt; I think it's about having the tools
to be a little bit more introspective.

00:20:30.210 --> 00:20:34.990
To examine the way that I go through the
world, my own biases and challenge them.

00:20:34.990 --> 00:20:39.780
And then really having the toolkit to do
that consistently and throughout my life.

00:20:39.780 --> 00:20:41.480
So that I know when I leave this place,

00:20:41.480 --> 00:20:45.183
I will be able to look back on
this experiences and drawn them.

00:20:45.183 --> 00:20:48.420
And maybe do a better job than
would have before business school.

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:51.440
&gt;&gt; That's great thank you,
I appreciate that.

00:20:53.040 --> 00:20:58.030
Here is my closing advice, this is for
all you applicants out there.

00:20:58.030 --> 00:21:01.720
I think what's most important
to me is to tell you do not

00:21:01.720 --> 00:21:04.730
underestimate your own potential.

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:06.460
There's no perfect applicant.

00:21:06.460 --> 00:21:10.480
There's no checklist that we're looking
at of attributes that we require.

00:21:10.480 --> 00:21:13.790
Decide for yourself whether
you're worth the investment

00:21:13.790 --> 00:21:16.350
to go through the application process.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:19.560
What I know from talking to many,
many applicants in the past.

00:21:19.560 --> 00:21:22.030
That you spending the time to know
what's most important to you,

00:21:22.030 --> 00:21:25.160
what you value, what your aspirations are.

00:21:25.160 --> 00:21:27.692
And then what you've really done and
accomplishments you've had,

00:21:27.692 --> 00:21:32.365
those are going to be critical to you at
this point in time, no matter what you do.

00:21:32.365 --> 00:21:35.105
We're genuinely excited
to learn about you,

00:21:35.105 --> 00:21:38.915
to learn about how you've actually
had impact on your teams,

00:21:38.915 --> 00:21:41.990
your organizations and
your communities and what you care about.

00:21:41.990 --> 00:21:44.830
So if you have a story to tell us,
you know that we're here and

00:21:44.830 --> 00:21:47.580
generally ready to and excited to read it.

00:21:47.580 --> 00:21:49.770
So thanks so much, Jessica,
for taking the time.

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:51.748
&gt;&gt; And thank you so much for
this, Kirsten, really.

00:21:51.748 --> 00:21:52.769
This was really helpful.

00:21:52.769 --> 00:21:57.850
[MUSIC]

